# Dashboard composition and editing

Editing changes the interface configuration; using a dashboard can operate devices. Make those contexts unmistakable. Preserve the existing cards, entity targets, visibility conditions and unsaved changes when appearance changes.

## Scope and native ownership

Native Sections views arrange cards in a grid and supply editing, placement and visibility features. This specification provides task and acceptance rules around the native editor; it does not add a civica layout editor or promise a keyboard alternative that has not been verified. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/sections/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-022**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| View mode | Show household controls and navigation. | Do not display reorder handles as device controls. |
| Edit mode | Name the dashboard and object being edited. | A move action must not activate the card’s primary device action. |
| Draft changed | Distinguish the edited configuration from the saved version. | Do not label a preview as saved. |
| Validation rejected | Associate the issue with its card/field and preserve supported draft data. | Do not discard unrelated card settings. |
| Save result uncertain | Keep the draft and inspect the native stored version. | Do not blindly overwrite a concurrent edit. |

## Integration contract

1. Let the native editor own its configuration and persistence. Theme changes must not serialise, reorder or rewrite saved dashboard records.
2. A card has a type, targets and layout/visibility configuration. Preserve unknown configuration fields when editing a supported subset.
3. State whether a change affects this view, a shared dashboard or a per-browser preference. Dashboard visibility is not a replacement for backend authorization.
4. Use native movement/resize mechanisms. Where drag is the only verified route, record that limitation and a supported editor alternative if one exists; CSS alone cannot make dragging keyboard accessible.

## Accessible interaction

- Label controls with their object, for example Edit kitchen light card. Maintain a logical focus order when a card moves or is removed.
- Check that dense placement does not make visual order contradict reading and keyboard order for the task.
- At 320px, enlarged text and touch zoom, keep save/cancel and validation reachable without overlaying a live device action.

## Failure and recovery

- If an entity disappears, retain its configured identifier and a useful invalid-target explanation rather than replacing the card silently.
- If a save is rejected, keep supported draft edits and native validation. After an uncertain response, compare against the actual stored version.
- Warn about the documented consequences of taking over an automatically maintained dashboard; do not promise a one-click reversal if unavailable.

